[{"text":"To compute chiral traces in the BMHV scheme, FeynCalc uses West’s formula. Still, NDR is the default scheme in FeynCalc.","anchor":"cb10-1","section":"Simplifications of Dirac matrix chains"},{"text":"In tree-level calculation a useful operation is the so-called SPVAT-decomposition of Dirac chains. This is done using DiracReduce","anchor":"cb10-1","section":"Simplifications of Dirac matrix chains"},{"text":"GA[\\[Mu], \\[Nu], \\[Rho]] . GS[p] . GA[\\[Alpha]] DiracReduce[%]","anchor":"cb11","section":"Simplifications of Dirac matrix chains"},{"text":"\\[\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\mu }.\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\nu }.\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\rho }.\\left(\\bar{\\gamma }\\cdot \\overline{p}\\right).\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\alpha }\\]","anchor":"cb11-2","section":"Simplifications of Dirac matrix chains"},{"text":"Gordon’s identities are implemented via GordonSimplify","anchor":"cb11-2","section":"Simplifications of Dirac matrix chains"},{"text":"SpinorUBar[p1, m1] . GA[\\[Mu]] . SpinorU[p2, m2] GordonSimplify[%]","anchor":"cb12","section":"Simplifications of Dirac matrix chains"},{"text":"\\[\\bar{u}(\\text{p1},\\text{m1}).\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\mu }.u(\\text{p2},\\text{m2})\\]","anchor":"cb12-2","section":"Simplifications of Dirac matrix chains"},{"text":"\\frac{\\left(\\overline{\\text{p1}}+\\overline{\\text{p2}}\\right)^{\\mu } \\left(\\varphi (\\overline{\\text{p1}},\\text{m1})\\right).\\left(\\varphi (\\overline{\\text{p2}},\\text{m2})\\right)}{\\text{m1}+\\text{m2}}+\\frac{i \\left(\\varphi (\\overline{\\text{p1}},\\text{m1})\\right).\\sigma ^{\\mu \\overline{\\text{p1}}-\\overline{\\text{p2}}}.\\left(\\varphi (\\overline{\\text{p2}},\\text{m2})\\right)}{\\text{m1}+\\text{m2}}","anchor":"cb12-2","section":"Simplifications of Dirac matrix chains"},{"text":"It is possible to reorder the free indices in a chain of Dirac matrices, which can sometimes help to simplify the expressions","anchor":"cb12-2","section":"Simplifications of Dirac matrix chains"},{"text":"DiracOrder[GA[\\[Mu], \\[Nu], \\[Rho]], {\\[Nu], \\[Mu]}]","anchor":"cb13","section":"Simplifications of Dirac matrix chains"},{"text":"\\[2 \\bar{\\gamma }^{\\rho } \\bar{g}^{\\mu \\nu }-\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\nu }.\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\mu }.\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\rho }\\]","anchor":"cb13-1","section":"Simplifications of Dirac matrix chains"},{"text":"However, since this procedure is computationally expensive, DiracSimplify will not apply it by default","anchor":"cb13-1","section":"Simplifications of Dirac matrix chains"},{"text":"DiracSimplify[GAD[\\[Mu], \\[Nu]] + GAD[\\[Nu], \\[Mu]]]","anchor":"cb14","section":"Simplifications of Dirac matrix chains"},{"text":"\\[\\gamma ^{\\mu }.\\gamma ^{\\nu }+\\gamma ^{\\nu }.\\gamma ^{\\mu }\\]","anchor":"cb14-1","section":"Simplifications of Dirac matrix chains"},{"text":"It can be activated via the option DiracOrder","anchor":"cb14-1","section":"Simplifications of Dirac matrix chains"},{"text":"DiracSimplify[GAD[\\[Mu], \\[Nu]] + GAD[\\[Nu], \\[Mu]], DiracOrder -> True]","anchor":"cb15","section":"Simplifications of Dirac matrix chains"},{"text":"\\[2 g^{\\mu \\nu }\\]","anchor":"cb15-1","section":"Simplifications of Dirac matrix chains"}]